## 10. REAL-TIME NOTIFICATION SYSTEM

Build a reliable persistent notification system.

Notifications must be generated from actual successful database events, not merely from frontend button clicks.

### Customer notifications

Notify customers when:

- An order is successfully placed.
- A supplier accepts an order.
- A supplier rejects an order.
- An order is being processed.
- An order is ready for delivery.
- An order is out for delivery.
- A supplier marks an order as delivered.
- Customer confirmation is required.
- A delivery problem is reported.
- An order is completed.
- A payment is verified or rejected, where applicable.

### Supplier notifications

Notify suppliers when:

- A new customer order is received.
- An order is cancelled.
- A customer reports a delivery problem.
- A customer confirms receipt.
- Admin changes an order requiring supplier attention.

### Admin notifications

Notify administrators when:

- New orders require attention.
- Suppliers reject orders.
- Delivery disputes are reported.
- Important payment events occur.
- Critical system synchronization failures occur.
- Important operational actions require review.

### Notification requirements

Each notification must contain:

- Unique notification ID.
- Recipient user ID.
- Related order ID, where applicable.
- Notification type.
- Title.
- Message.
- Created timestamp.
- Read/unread status.
- Related application navigation destination.
- Optional action metadata.

Implement:

- Real-time notification updates.
- Unread notification counters.
- Notification history.
- Mark as read.
- Mark all as read.
- Relevant notification actions.
- Persistent storage in Firestore.
- Retry handling for failed notification processing.
- Protection against duplicate notifications.

Notifications must remain available after logout and login.

A notification must not disappear merely because the user refreshed the page.

Separate in-app notifications from optional push notifications.

If Firebase Cloud Messaging is already available, preserve and improve it. If not, inspect the existing architecture before adding it.

Do not claim push notifications work until browser/mobile permissions and actual delivery have been tested.

